Major Pass 2 correctness fixes
Four discoveries were particularly important.
1. Candidate versus final verification
An early design risk allowed BAN's successful internal candidate verification to become confused with final correctness.
This was corrected to:
BAN candidate
    ↓
objective candidate verification
    ↓
selection/recovery
    ↓
final answer realization
    ↓
independent final-answer verification
Final accuracy is based on the delivered answer, not merely on an internal correct thought.
2. Incomplete Ollama streams
A stream ending without the required completion indication could previously appear clean.
Pass 2 changed that to an explicit transport failure.
3. Timeout harness hang
A mock timeout server exposed a harness hang. The harness was corrected instead of weakening real timeout semantics.
4. Dataset duplicate detection
The first repetitive 100-case draft was rejected by the duplicate detector.
The detector was not weakened.
The dataset generator was corrected instead.
That is a useful example of the development philosophy already becoming embedded in BAN.
Verification
Experimental verification supported:
normalized exact values
numeric tolerance
safe numeric constraints
structured JSON invariants
The experimental path had no permissive fallback.
Analysis
Pass 2 added McNemar paired analysis and defined recovery as BAN success after the initial top branch failed objective verification. It explicitly refused to fabricate an equivalent baseline recovery probability because baseline does not possess the same internal branch structure.
Quality gates
Recorded Pass 2 state:
go test -timeout 90s ./...
PASS

go vet ./...
PASS

ARM64 build
PASS
The live Ollama endpoint was unavailable, so no superiority claim was made and no experimental result was fabricated.
That principle is also explicitly stated in the repository documentation: no claim of improvement is valid until live paired results support it.



