The first three passes as one architecture
This is the documentation-level progression I would preserve:
PASS 1
POSSIBILITY
────────────────────────────────
Don't assume one reasoning path.

Input
  ↓
multiple possibilities
  ↓
directed graph
  ↓
challenge / retain / recover
  ↓
candidate


PASS 2
EXPERIMENT
────────────────────────────────
Does the architecture actually help?

Frozen model
  ↓
Baseline vs BAN
  ↓
same task/configuration
  ↓
objective paired verification
  ↓
measured comparative outcome


PASS 3
REALITY
────────────────────────────────
What does "correct" actually mean?

Claim
  ↓
measurement contract
  ↓
observation/evidence
  ↓
SUPPORTED / CONTRADICTED /
INCONCLUSIVE / ERROR
  ↓
measurement-grounded correctness
Which leads directly into the Pass 4 we already started:
PASS 4
MEMORY
────────────────────────────────
Can prior experience improve the possibility
space without becoming self-created truth?

Memory
  ↓
guides possibility generation
  ↓
BAN search
  ↓
current measurement
  ↓
reinforce / revise / supersede memory
That gives the first four passes a very clean conceptual progression:
Possibility → Experiment → Reality → Memory
